      <div class="content-sec">
        <h2>What is CDOM & fDOM?</h2>
        <p><strong>CDOM (Chromophoric Dissolved Organic Matter)</strong> absorbs UV and visible light, giving natural waters a tea-like yellowish-brown hue. <strong>fDOM (Fluorescent Dissolved Organic Matter)</strong> is the fraction of CDOM that fluoresces when excited by UV light, enabling real-time optical tracking of organic carbon.</p>
        
        <h2>Ecosystem & Health Significance</h2>
        <p>CDOM controls light penetration in aquatic ecosystems, shielding organisms from harmful UV rays while simultaneously limiting photosynthetic activity. In drinking water utilities, high CDOM levels indicate potential precursors for carcinogenic disinfection byproducts (trihalomethanes/THMs) during chlorination.</p>
        
        <h2>Monitoring Technologies</h2>
        <p>Modern in-situ optical fluorometers excite water samples at ~370 nm wavelength and measure fluorescence emission at ~460 nm to deliver continuous, real-time TOC estimates without liquid reagents.</p>
      </div>

